{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VttoYpTC4VDWu2iZaNrHt3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tvenue: CoRL 2026\n2\tabstract_ddl: 2026-05-26\n3\tpaper_ddl: 2026-05-29\n4\tformat: \"8 pages + references\"\n5\t\n6\ttitle_candidates:\n7\t  - \"Where Do Action Representations Come From? Dissecting Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n8\t  - \"Self-Supervised Geometry is King: Representational Analysis of Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n9\t  - \"Vision Encodes, Language Routes: A Representational Decomposition of VLA Action Knowledge\"\n10\t\n11\t# ============================================================\n12\t# SECTIONS\n13\t# ============================================================\n14\tsections:\n15\t\n16\t  - name: Abstract\n17\t    core_evidence:\n18\t      - d041_method_b_rsa            # DINO cls=0.692 highest, full per-layer curves\n19\t      - plan_013_branch_split        # DINOv2 branch=0.619, SigLIP branch=0.621\n20\t      - plan_012_clip_vit_control    # CLIP RSA=0.557, R²=0.587\n21\t      - unified_action_probe         # R² unified hyperparams\n22\t      - d041_training_effect_bootstrap  # best-vs-best Δ=+0.017 n.s., same-layer Δ=+0.079\n23\t    key_numbers:\n24\t      - \"DINO cls RSA=0.692 (highest); Cross-encoder: DINO patch_mean (0.631) > SigLIP+Proj (0.615) > CLIP (0.557). Self-supervised geometry is king.\"\n25\t      - \"R²: DINO (0.634) ≈ Proj (0.630) > CLIP (0.587). last_preaction R²=0.671 (readout concentration, p=0.002 vs Proj).\"\n26\t      - \"LLM inverted-U: L0=0.612 → L12=0.628 (peak, above projector 0.615) → L32=0.422.\"\n27\t      - \"Training peak shift: best-vs-best Δ=+0.017 n.s.; same-layer L12 Δ=+0.079 p<0.0001.\"\n28\t      - \"Instruction invariance: max |Δ|=0.006.\"\n29\t\n30\t  - name: Introduction\n31\t    structure:\n32\t      - para_1_problem: >\n33\t          VLAs achieve impressive manipulation performance by fine-tuning LLMs on robot data.\n34\t          Implicit assumption: fine-tuning injects action-relevant representational knowledge.\n35\t          But where do action representations actually come from?\n36\t      - para_2_gap: >\n37\t          Grant et al. 2026 showed visual pathway dominates at behavioral level.\n38\t          Hou 2026 / Fei 2025 showed VLAs largely ignore language instructions.\n39\t          But no work has performed representational decomposition — measuring each component's\n40\t          independent contribution to action-relevant structure, nor traced action geometry\n41\t          through the LLM's 33 layers.\n42\t      - para_3_approach: >\n43\t          We systematically decompose OpenVLA: extract representations from\n44\t          (1) standalone vision encoders (DINOv2, CLIP), (2) fused backbone + projector,\n45\t          (3) LLM at every layer (image_mean + last_preaction), comparing trained vs untrained\n46\t          LLM × instruction conditions. RSA (geometric alignment), CKA (global structure),\n47\t          and functional probes (action prediction R²).\n48\t      - para_4_findings: >\n49\t          Three findings: (1) Self-supervised DINOv2 provides strongest action-aligned geometry\n50\t          (cls RSA=0.692), outperforming VL-pretrained CLIP (0.557). VL pretraining does not\n51\t          enhance action-relevant structure. (2) LLM transformation is non-monotonic:\n52\t          first 12 layers enhance geometric alignment (0.612→0.628, surpassing projector 0.615),\n53\t          final 20 layers degrade it (→0.422). CKA monotonically rises, revealing metric-level\n54\t          dissociation. (3) Training redistributes action signal (peak shift L0→L12) without\n55\t          creating new knowledge (best-vs-best Δ=+0.017, n.s.). Instruction content has no\n56\t          effect (|Δ|<0.006).\n57\t      - para_5_implications: >\n58\t          Implications for VLA design: vision encoder pretraining quality (especially\n59\t          self-supervised) is the primary lever; LLM's early layers enhance geometry while\n60\t          late layers degrade it; training teaches redistribution not creation.\n61\t    core_evidence:\n62\t      - d041_method_b_rsa\n63\t      - plan_012_clip_vit_control\n64\t      - plan_013_branch_split\n65\t      - unified_action_probe\n66\t      - d041_training_effect_bootstrap\n67\t\n68\t  - name: Related Work\n69\t    categories:\n70\t      - name: \"Vision-Language-Action Models\"\n71\t        papers:\n72\t          - \"Karamcheti et al. 2024 (Prismatic/OpenVLA) — architectural foundation\"\n73\t          - \"Kim et al. 2024 (OpenVLA) — model we analyze\"\n74\t          - \"Brohan et al. 2023 (RT-2) — VLA paradigm origin\"\n75\t      - name: \"Behavioral Analysis of VLAs\"\n76\t        papers:\n77\t          - \"Grant et al. 2026 — visual pathway dominance (behavioral level)\"\n78\t          - \"Hou 2026 (LangGap) — VLAs ignore language instructions\"\n79\t          - \"Fei 2025 (LIBERO-Plus) — language-conditioned evaluation\"\n80\t      - name: \"Mechanistic Interpretability of VLAs\"\n81\t        papers:\n82\t          - \"Haon et al. CoRL 2025 — activation steering on OpenVLA\"\n83\t          - \"Swann et al. 2026 — SAE-based OpenVLA interpretability\"\n84\t          - \"Rizzardo et al. 2026 — feature observability/controllability\"\n85\t          - \"Molinari et al. 2025 — emergent world representations\"\n86\t          - \"Lu et al. 2025 — linear probing OpenVLA hidden layers\"\n87\t      - name: \"Vision Encoder Representations\"\n88\t        papers:\n89\t          - \"Fang 2025 — fine-tuning erodes VLM reasoning\"\n90\t          - \"Oquab et al. 2024 (DINOv2) — self-supervised vision baseline\"\n91\t          - \"Zhai et al. 2023 (SigLIP) — vision-language pretraining\"\n92\t      - name: \"Representational Similarity Analysis\"\n93\t        papers:\n94\t          - \"Kriegeskorte et al. 2008 — RSA framework\"\n95\t          - \"Kornblith et al. 2019 — CKA\"\n96\t    positioning: >\n97\t      Grant et al. 2026 is closest — we confirm their behavioral finding at representation level\n98\t      with quantitative component decomposition + per-layer curves. Key difference: they find\n99\t      \"visual pathway dominates,\" we further show self-supervised (DINO) > VL-pretrained (CLIP)\n100\t      within the visual pathway. Lu et al. 2025 methodologically closest — we differentiate via\n101\t      component-level (not just layer-level) analysis, standalone encoder comparisons, and causal\n102\t      controls (trained/untrained × instruction ablation). Haon et al. same venue+model —\n103\t      complementary (sparse semantic directions vs geometric analysis).\n104\t\n105\t  - name: Method\n106\t    subsections:\n107\t      - name: \"2.1 Model and Data\"\n108\t        content_outline: >\n109\t          OpenVLA architecture: DinoSigLIP fused backbone (DINOv2 ViT-L 1024d + SigLIP-SO400M\n110\t          1152d → torch.cat → 2176d) → MLP projector (2176→4096) → Llama-2 7B (33 layers)\n111\t          → action head. Base OpenVLA-7B checkpoint (OXE-pretrained on 970K episodes),\n112\t          NOT fine-tuned on LIBERO. \"Trained\" = OXE-pretrained LLM weights;\n113\t          \"Untrained\" = LLM weights randomly reinitialized (vision encoder + projector\n114\t          identical, cos_sim=1.0). LIBERO-Goal benchmark: 10 tasks, 50 demos/task.\n115\t      - name: \"2.2 Representation Extraction\"\n116\t        content_outline: >\n117\t          Ten representation sources spanning three levels:\n118\t          (a) Standalone encoders: DINOv2 cls_token, DINOv2 patch_mean, CLIP ViT-L/14 cls_token;\n119\t          (b) Fused backbone: DinoSigLIP 2176d (+ branch-split: DINOv2 1024d, SigLIP 1152d);\n120\t          (c) After projector: SigLIP+Projector 4096d;\n121\t          (d) LLM per-layer: image_mean (mean of image tokens at each of 33 layers),\n122\t              last_preaction (readout token = last token before action prediction);\n123\t          2×2 conditions for LLM: trained/untrained × with-instruction/no-instruction.\n124\t      - name: \"2.3 Representational Similarity Analysis (RSA)\"\n125\t        content_outline: >\n126\t          Method B: within-task pairing + euclidean action distance + full trajectory,\n127\t          50 demos/task. Construct RDMs for representations and 7-D action space.\n128\t          Spearman correlation between upper-triangular RDM vectors.\n129\t          Per-task RSA aggregated across tasks.\n130\t          Bootstrap CI (10,000 resamples) for pairwise comparisons.\n131\t      - name: \"2.4 Functional Action Probes\"\n132\t        content_outline: >\n133\t          Unified hyperparams: MLP probe (Linear→ReLU→Linear, input_dim→256→7;\n134\t          Adam lr=1e-3, batch=256, epochs=50, early stopping patience=5, seed=42)\n135\t          trained on representation→action mapping. Mean R² across 7 action dimensions.\n136\t          Per-task train/val splits. Bootstrap paired comparison for statistical significance.\n137\t          Also: action-delta probes (predict Δaction = a_{t+1} - a_t).\n138\t          All R² from unified_action_probe with standardized hyperparameters.\n139\t      - name: \"2.5 CKA Analysis\"\n140\t        content_outline: >\n141\t          Linear CKA and RDM-based CKA as complementary metric to RSA.\n142\t          Motivation: RSA captures local geometric alignment, CKA captures global structure.\n143\t          Per-layer CKA curves to compare with RSA inverted-U trajectory.\n144\t\n145\t  - name: Experiments\n146\t    subsections:\n147\t      - name: \"3.1 Experimental Setup\"\n148\t        content_outline: >\n149\t          LIBERO-Goal benchmark: 10 manipulation tasks, 50 demos each.\n150\t          Method B RSA protocol (within-task + euclidean + full trajectory).\n151\t          Representation extraction pipeline. Statistical testing protocol\n152\t          (bootstrap 10K, permutation). Hardware: 2× GPU on westd-19183.\n153\t\n154\t      - name: \"3.2 Vision Encoder Dominance (Claim 1)\"\n155\t        core_evidence:\n156\t          - d041_method_b_rsa            # DINO cls=0.692, patch_mean=0.631\n157\t          - plan_012_clip_vit_control    # CLIP RSA=0.557, R²=0.587\n158\t          - plan_013_branch_split        # DINOv2 branch=0.619, SigLIP branch=0.621, Fused=0.634\n159\t          - unified_action_probe         # R²: DINO 0.634 ≈ Proj 0.630 > CLIP 0.587\n160\t        key_numbers:\n161\t          - \"DINO cls_token RSA=0.692 — highest across all feature sources\"\n162\t          - \"Cross-encoder ranking: DINO patch_mean (0.631) > SigLIP+Proj (0.615) > CLIP (0.557)\"\n163\t          - \"Branch-split: DINOv2 1024d (0.619) ≈ SigLIP 1152d (0.621); Fused 2176d (0.634) > either branch\"\n164\t          - \"Projector compression: Fused 0.634 → Proj 0.615 (MLP 2176→4096)\"\n165\t          - \"R² ranking: DINO (0.634) ≈ Proj (0.630) > CLIP (0.587); RSA and R² orderings agree\"\n166\t          - \"Self-supervised DINOv2 > VL-pretrained CLIP in both geometry and function → VL pretraining does not enhance action structure\"\n167\t        structure: >\n168\t          (a) Cross-encoder RSA: DINO cls (0.692) > DINO patch_mean (0.631) > SigLIP+Proj (0.615)\n169\t              > CLIP (0.557). Self-supervised DINO dominates, VL pretraining does not help.\n170\t          (b) Branch-split: DinoSigLIP fused (0.634) has roughly equal contributions from\n171\t              DINOv2 branch (0.619) and SigLIP branch (0.621). Projector MLP compresses 0.634→0.615.\n172\t          (c) Functional probe: R² ordering consistent — DINO (0.634) ≈ Proj (0.630) > CLIP (0.587).\n173\t              RSA and R² rankings agree; no geometric-functional dissociation.\n174\t          (d) CLIP as VL-pretraining control: CLIP (0.557) < DINO (0.631/0.692),\n175\t              confirming VL pretraining is NOT the source of action-relevant structure.\n176\t\n177\t      - name: \"3.3 LLM Non-Monotonic Transformation (Claim 2)\"\n178\t        core_evidence:\n179\t          - d041_method_b_rsa            # Per-layer inverted-U curve\n180\t          - plan_009_cka_verification    # CKA monotonic rise, metric dissociation\n181\t          - unified_action_probe         # last_preaction R²=0.671 > Proj 0.630\n182\t        key_numbers:\n183\t          - \"Trained image_mean inverted-U: L0=0.612 → L12=0.628 (peak) → L32=0.422\"\n184\t          - \"L12 peak (0.628) > projector input (0.615) — first 12 layers enhance geometry\"\n185\t          - \"Untrained image_mean monotonic decay: L0=0.612 → L12=0.550 → L32=0.545\"\n186\t          - \"CKA monotonic rise: L0=0.081 → L32=0.098 (global structure preserved)\"\n187\t          - \"RSA inverted-U vs CKA monotonic rise → metric-level dissociation\"\n188\t          - \"Readout concentration: trained last_preaction peak L12 RSA=0.643\"\n189\t          - \"Functional: last_preaction R²=0.671 > projector 0.630 (p=0.002)\"\n190\t          - \"image_mean R²=0.599 < projector 0.630 (p<0.001)\"\n191\t        structure: >\n192\t          (a) Per-layer RSA curve: trained image_mean shows inverted-U — first 12 layers enhance\n193\t              geometric alignment beyond projector input (0.612→0.628>0.615), last 20 layers\n194\t              degrade (→0.422). Untrained shows monotonic decay from L0.\n195\t          (b) Enhancement is training-dependent: only trained model shows inverted-U; untrained\n196\t              model decays monotonically from L0=0.612.\n197\t          (c) Readout concentration: trained last_preaction RSA=0.643 at L12, surpassing\n198\t              all image_mean values. Functional probe confirms: last_preaction R²=0.671 > Proj 0.630 (p=0.002).\n199\t          (d) CKA complement: CKA monotonically rises L0→L32, while RSA has inverted-U.\n200\t              LLM changes local geometric alignment while preserving global structure.\n201\t              Metric-level dissociation — interpretation depends on metric choice.\n202\t\n203\t      - name: \"3.4 Training Redistributes, Doesn't Create (Claim 3)\"\n204\t        core_evidence:\n205\t          - d041_training_effect_bootstrap  # Best-vs-best and same-layer comparisons\n206\t          - d041_method_b_rsa               # Peak shift L0→L12\n207\t          - plan_008_random_inst_control    # Instruction invariance\n208\t          - plan_004_output_head_analysis   # Vision backbone identical\n209\t        key_numbers:\n210\t          - \"Peak shift: untrained peaks at L0, trained peaks at L12\"\n211\t          - \"Best-vs-best (trained L12 vs untrained L0): Δ=+0.017, CI includes 0, n.s.\"\n212\t          - \"Same-layer L12: trained 0.628 vs untrained 0.550, Δ=+0.079, p<0.0001\"\n213\t          - \"Instruction invariance image_mean: max |Δ|=0.006 (normal/random/shuffled/empty)\"\n214\t          - \"Instruction invariance last_preaction: max |Δ|=0.017\"\n215\t          - \"Vision backbone + projector: identical weights (cos_sim=1.0) between trained/untrained\"\n216\t        structure: >\n217\t          (a) Peak shift: training moves RSA peak from L0 (untrained) to L12 (trained).\n218\t              This is redistribution of action signal across layers, not invariance.\n219\t          (b) Best-vs-best comparison: peak amplitudes are comparable (Δ=+0.017, n.s.) —\n220\t              training doesn't create new action-relevant structure, it redistributes.\n221\t          (c) Same-layer L12: Δ=+0.079, p<0.0001 — training concentrates signal\n222\t              at L12, which untrained model distributes differently.\n223\t          (d) Instruction invariance: max |Δ|=0.006 for image_mean, 0.017 for last_preaction.\n224\t              Instruction content does not modulate representation structure.\n225\t          (e) Functional confirmation: last_preaction R²=0.671 > Proj 0.630 (p=0.002) —\n226\t              training teaches readout concentration.\n227\t\n228\t      - name: \"3.5 Metric Sensitivity: RSA vs CKA (Supplementary or Main)\"\n229\t        core_evidence:\n230\t          - plan_009_cka_verification\n231\t        key_numbers:\n232\t          - \"Linear CKA: untrained(0.089) > trained(0.083) > proj(0.081) > DINO(0.066)\"\n233\t          - \"CKA per-layer trained image_mean: L0=0.081 → L32=0.098 — monotonic rise\"\n234\t          - \"CKA last_preaction: L32=0.127 (highest)\"\n235\t          - \"RSA inverted-U (peak L12) vs CKA monotonic rise — metric dissociation\"\n236\t        structure: >\n237\t          RSA captures local geometric alignment, CKA captures global structure.\n238\t          RSA inverted-U vs CKA monotonic rise reveals LLM changes geometry while preserving\n239\t          global structure. CKA encoder ordering reverses RSA ordering.\n240\t          CKA last_preaction highest (0.127) — consistent with readout concentration.\n241\t          Could be main text subsection or supplementary depending on space.\n242\t\n243\t  - name: Discussion\n244\t    structure:\n245\t      - para_1_synthesis: >\n246\t          Three findings form coherent picture: self-supervised vision encoder provides\n247\t          strongest action-aligned geometry (DINO cls 0.692), LLM non-monotonically transforms\n248\t          it (enhance first 12 layers, degrade last 20), training redistributes signal\n249\t          (peak shift L0→L12) without creating new knowledge.\n250\t      - para_2_self_supervised_dominance: >\n251\t          DINO (0.692/0.631) > CLIP (0.557) overturns the assumption that VL pretraining\n252\t          helps action representations. Self-supervised geometric learning (DINOv2's\n253\t          patch-level objectives) produces better action-aligned structure than\n254\t          vision-language contrastive learning. R² confirms: DINO (0.634) ≈ Proj (0.630) > CLIP (0.587).\n255\t      - para_3_branch_split_insight: >\n256\t          Branch-split reveals both branches contribute roughly equally (DINOv2 0.619, SigLIP 0.621)\n257\t          to the fused backbone (0.634). Projector MLP compresses rather than enhances (0.634→0.615).\n258\t          The standalone DINO cls_token (0.692) exceeding the fused backbone (0.634) suggests\n259\t          SigLIP concatenation may dilute DINOv2's geometric structure.\n260\t      - para_4_llm_transformation: >\n261\t          The inverted-U trajectory (enhance→degrade) is more nuanced than simple degradation.\n262\t          First 12 layers actively enhance geometric alignment beyond projector input\n263\t          (0.612→0.628>0.615), paralleling NLP findings that middle layers are most task-informative.\n264\t          CKA monotonic rise alongside RSA inverted-U shows LLM transforms local geometry\n265\t          while preserving global structure — metric choice determines narrative.\n266\t      - para_5_implications: >\n267\t          (1) Vision encoder pretraining quality is primary lever — self-supervised > VL-pretrained.\n268\t          (2) LLM early layers beneficial; consider early-exit for action extraction.\n269\t          (3) Training teaches redistribution (peak shift) not knowledge creation.\n270\t          (4) Language instructions do not modulate representation structure (|Δ|<0.006).\n271\t      - para_6_connection_to_behavioral: >\n272\t          Our representational findings explain Grant et al. 2026's behavioral results:\n273\t          vision dominates because the frozen encoder provides the action-relevant structure.\n274\t          We go further: within vision, self-supervised (DINO) > VL-pretrained (CLIP).\n275\t          \"Encoded but not used\" (their instruction finding) aligns with our instruction invariance.\n276\t      - para_7_limitations: >\n277\t          Single architecture (OpenVLA). Single benchmark suite (LIBERO-Goal).\n278\t          RSA captures structural alignment, not causal contribution.\n279\t          R² peak at L19 vs RSA peak at L12 — functional capacity may accumulate\n280\t          after geometric alignment begins declining (plan_011 pending).\n281\t          \"Trained\" = OXE-pretrained, not task-specific fine-tuned.\n282\t    core_evidence:\n283\t      - d041_method_b_rsa\n284\t      - plan_012_clip_vit_control\n285\t      - plan_013_branch_split\n286\t      - unified_action_probe\n287\t      - plan_009_cka_verification\n288\t\n289\t  - name: Conclusion\n290\t    structure:\n291\t      - para_1_summary: >\n292\t          First systematic representational decomposition of a VLA. Three findings:\n293\t          (1) self-supervised vision encoder (DINOv2) provides strongest action-aligned geometry,\n294\t          outperforming VL-pretrained encoders — VL pretraining does not enhance action structure;\n295\t          (2) LLM non-monotonically transforms action geometry — first 12 layers enhance,\n296\t          last 20 layers degrade, while global structure (CKA) is preserved;\n297\t          (3) training redistributes action signal across layers (peak shift L0→L12)\n298\t          without creating new knowledge.\n299\t      - para_2_takeaway: >\n300\t          Action representations in VLAs are inherited from the vision encoder, not learned\n301\t          by the LLM. Design implications: invest in self-supervised vision encoder pretraining;\n302\t          LLM's early-layer enhancement suggests architectural opportunities;\n303\t          instruction content's null effect on representations demands rethinking language's role in VLAs.\n304\t\n305\t# ============================================================\n306\t# FIGURES\n307\t# ============================================================\n308\tfigures:\n309\t  - id: fig1\n310\t    type: \"概念图/架构图\"\n311\t    description: >\n312\t      VLA pipeline decomposition diagram. Shows DinoSigLIP fused backbone\n313\t      (DINOv2 ViT-L + SigLIP-SO400M → torch.cat → 2176d) → MLP Projector (→4096d)\n314\t      → Llama-2 7B (33 layers) → Action Head.\n315\t      Annotate extraction points: (a) standalone encoders (DINOv2, CLIP),\n316\t      (b) fused backbone + branch-split, (c) projector output,\n317\t      (d) per-layer image_mean, (e) per-layer last_preaction.\n318\t      Show 2×2 conditions (trained/untrained × with/no instruction).\n319\t      Inset: RSA (within-task, euclidean) and functional probe methodology schematic.\n320\t    data_source: \"architectural (no experiment data)\"\n321\t    placement: \"Section 2 or Section 1\"\n322\t\n323\t  - id: fig2\n324\t    type: \"实验结果图 — Bar chart\"\n325\t    description: >\n326\t      Main cross-encoder RSA comparison. Bars ordered by RSA:\n327\t      DINO cls (0.692), Fused DinoSigLIP (0.634), DINO patch_mean (0.631),\n328\t      SigLIP+Proj (0.615), CLIP (0.557).\n329\t      Visual grouping: self-supervised (DINO variants) >> VL-pretrained (CLIP).\n330\t      Branch-split inset: DINOv2 branch (0.619) ≈ SigLIP branch (0.621).\n331\t      Error bars from bootstrap CI.\n332\t    data_source:\n333\t      - d041_method_b_rsa\n334\t      - plan_012_clip_vit_control\n335\t      - plan_013_branch_split\n336\t    placement: \"Section 3.2\"\n337\t\n338\t  - id: fig3\n339\t    type: \"实验结果图 — Line plot\"\n340\t    description: >\n341\t      Per-layer RSA curves (image_mean). X-axis: Projector → L0 → L1 → ... → L32.\n342\t      Y-axis: RSA with action space. Two primary lines: trained (inverted-U,\n343\t      peak L12=0.628) and untrained (monotonic decay from L0=0.612).\n344\t      Horizontal reference lines: projector (0.615), DINO cls (0.692), CLIP (0.557).\n345\t      Key annotation: trained L12 (0.628) exceeds projector (0.615).\n346\t      Optional: last_preaction curves (trained peak L12=0.643, untrained peak L1=0.626).\n347\t    data_source:\n348\t      - d041_method_b_rsa\n349\t    placement: \"Section 3.3\"\n350\t\n351\t  - id: fig4\n352\t    type: \"实验结果图 — Grouped bar chart\"\n353\t    description: >\n354\t      Functional probe R² comparison (unified_action_probe, standardized hyperparams).\n355\t      Bars: last_preaction (0.671), DINO cls (0.634), Projector (0.630),\n356\t      image_mean (0.599), CLIP (0.587).\n357\t      Shows: (a) last_preaction > all (readout concentration),\n358\t      (b) DINO ≈ Proj > CLIP (consistent with RSA ordering),\n359\t      (c) image_mean < Proj (distributed degradation).\n360\t      Error bars + significance brackets for key comparisons.\n361\t    data_source:\n362\t      - unified_action_probe\n363\t    placement: \"Section 3.3\"\n364\t\n365\t  - id: fig5\n366\t    type: \"实验结果图 — Overlay plot with peak annotations\"\n367\t    description: >\n368\t      Training effect: peak shift visualization. Trained vs untrained per-layer\n369\t      RSA curves (image_mean) overlaid. Trained: peaks L12 (0.628).\n370\t      Untrained: peaks L0 (0.612).\n371\t      Annotations: best-vs-best Δ=+0.017 (n.s., CI includes 0) between peaks;\n372\t      same-layer L12 Δ=+0.079 (p<0.0001) vertical gap annotation.\n373\t      Visual message: training shifts WHERE the peak is, not HOW HIGH it is.\n374\t    data_source:\n375\t      - d041_method_b_rsa\n376\t      - d041_training_effect_bootstrap\n377\t    placement: \"Section 3.4\"\n378\t\n379\t  - id: fig6\n380\t    type: \"实验结果图 — Dual-panel plot (supplementary candidate)\"\n381\t    description: >\n382\t      RSA vs CKA per-layer comparison. Panel A: RSA inverted-U (peak L12=0.628,\n383\t      endpoint L32=0.422). Panel B: CKA monotonic rise (L0=0.081 → L32=0.098).\n384\t      Shows metric-level dissociation: local geometry degrades while global\n385\t      structure is preserved. CKA last_preaction (0.127) as highest point.\n386\t    data_source:\n387\t      - plan_009_cka_verification\n388\t      - d041_method_b_rsa\n389\t    placement: \"Section 3.5 or Supplementary\"\n390\t\n391\t# ============================================================\n392\t# TABLES\n393\t# ============================================================\n394\ttables:\n395\t  - id: tab1\n396\t    description: \"Main cross-encoder RSA results (Method B: within-task + euclidean + full trajectory)\"\n397\t    columns: [\"Model / Feature Source\", \"Aggregation\", \"RSA\", \"Notes\"]\n398\t    rows_outline:\n399\t      - \"DINO cls_token | cl